.TH "UPTERM" "1" "Oct 2026" "Upterm 0.0.0+dev" "Upterm Manual"

.SH NAME
upterm-session-wait - Wait for a session to end


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBupterm session wait NAME|ID [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Wait for a session to end, given its name or session ID, and exit with its
outcome.

.PP
NAME|ID is the session's name (the one 'upterm host' printed, or --name
gave it) or its session ID; 'upterm session list' shows both.

.PP
Given a name, it waits for the session that holds the name when it starts,
so let 'upterm host --detach' return before you wait, as in the example. A
session that has already ended is reported from its record, which
\&'upterm session list' deletes 7 days after it ended.

.PP
Exit status follows the session: its command's status when the command
exited; 0 when it was stopped, or no guest joined within its join timeout;
128+N when the host or its command was killed by signal N; 125 when the
outcome can't be known or read, or no session has the name. A command can
exit 125 too; 'upterm session info NAME -o json' has the reason. Ctrl-C
stops waiting with 130 and leaves the session running.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for wait


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Enable debug logging (log file: /home/user/.local/state/upterm/upterm.log). A non-empty DEBUG turns it on too.


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # Wait for a detached session and take its exit status:
  upterm host --detach --accept --name build -- make
  upterm session wait build

  # Wait for a session given its session ID:
  upterm session wait d3k5fia7m4us73ehblug
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBupterm-session(1)\fP


